  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=\u27e88,4,6\u27e9,$ $b=\u27e88,-4,5\u27e9,$ and $c=\u27e80,2,k\u27e9$ are coplanar.",
  "worked_solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}|+6\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8(-4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 5)-4(8\\cdot k-0\\cdot 5)+6(8\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-32k-80-32k+96 \\\\  & =16-64k \\\\  & =16(1-4k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 16(1-4k) & =0 \\\\ 1-4k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$",
